#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158151} :activity/id #uuid "a2778177-99cd-5d9a-9c00-3f85c8361e9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775 :topic/id #uuid "65bfe855-0363-58b6-a91d-f3f12be3a932" :topic/math-academy-id 2836} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157301 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157105 :question/id #uuid "77354dd3-3f68-5d8e-b9db-3ef2dd0d1e5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7417"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f82e2-e564-522b-a46b-08313bf1aaf1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368831}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Constant"} :step/id #uuid "2d9fc28e-a025-50d3-aadd-74b750d2c00f" :step/math-academy-id 27503 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157302 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157106 :question/id #uuid "570b8071-d445-5782-b559-4fc220da50d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ac63c-b20a-55fd-a1b5-29400355f195" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385817}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "5d6c4425-e2ac-54f8-89eb-4c5834d4d3ec" :step/math-academy-id 27504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158488 :tutorial/content "There are several important properties that we can use to simplify expressions involving expected values.\n\nThe first property is that the expected value of a constant is just the constant itself. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have $$\\textrm E[a] = a.$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\textrm E[5] = 5,\\qquad \\textrm E[-99] = -99,\\qquad \\textrm E[\\sqrt 2] = \\sqrt 2.$$\n\nThe idea that the expected value of a constant is equal to the same constant makes intuitive sense. If $a$ is a constant, then it does not vary. Therefore, it is always sure to have the same value." :tutorial/id #uuid "4b67be84-b702-5707-9433-0dbd49d9264b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4497 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8243818e-fa41-5d84-a795-11bc994eb0ce" :step/math-academy-id 27700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158489 :tutorial/content "We can also factor constants outside of an expected value expression. So for any constant $a$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X].$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\textrm E[5X] = 5 \\textrm E[X],\\qquad\\textrm E[-9X] = -9 \\textrm E[X], \\qquad \\textrm E[\\pi X] = \\pi \\textrm E[X].$$\n\nWe can prove this rule using the definition of the expected value, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[aX] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}ax\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & =a{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & =a\\text{E}[X]\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "888aac3a-696d-5c89-8091-757e3f8db86b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4506 :tutorial/title "The Expected Value of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "b1582c01-ddd7-525e-a721-192543f80a68" :step/math-academy-id 27747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158146}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158546 :tutorial/content "Finally, we can combine the factor and constant rules into one simple rule.\n\nSuppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. By combining our two rules, we have\n\n$$\\textrm E[aX + b] = a\\textrm E[X] + b.$$\n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4,$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+2] & =\\text{E}[3X+2] \\\\ & =3\\text{E}[X]+2 \\\\ & =3(4)+2 \\\\ & =12+2 \\\\ & =14.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f861e35c-2827-5655-9b71-c93c4741794b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5452 :tutorial/title "The Expected Value of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "5d5b5303-4eae-5908-bc31-6eae5cf5c87e" :step/math-academy-id 33938 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157204 :question/id #uuid "a92e44a1-5eaf-5dab-b1aa-83ba4e58cf3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5449da36-9aae-591d-bab6-7ed55d7d9d4b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382888}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "f16d6f2c-1c33-51ea-9e4e-3ffdc289dae2" :step/math-academy-id 34021}] :activity/title "Properties of Expectation for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157491}]}
